🚀 Full-Stack Insurance Pricing System
This project is a technical assessment consisting of a Spring Boot 3 backend (Pricing Engine) and an Angular 18 frontend (Quote Management). It allows users to calculate insurance quotes based on product, age, and geographical zone.

🛠️ Tech Stack
Backend: Java 17, Spring Boot 3, Spring Data JPA, H2 Database (In-memory), Maven.

Frontend: Angular 18 (Standalone Components), Reactive Forms, RxJS, CSS3.

🏗️ Getting Started
1. Backend Setup (Spring Boot)
The backend manages product data and calculates pricing based on specific business rules.

Navigate to the backend folder.

Configure CORS: Ensure ProductController and QuoteController have @CrossOrigin(origins = "http://localhost:4200").

Run the application:

Bash
mvn spring-boot:run
Database Console: Access the H2 console at http://localhost:8080/h2-console (JDBC URL: jdbc:h2:mem:testdb).

API Endpoints:

GET /api/products: List available insurance products.

POST /api/quotes: Calculate and save a new quote.

GET /api/quotes: Retrieve all calculated quotes.

2. Frontend Setup (Angular)
The frontend provides a modern UI for agents to select products and generate quotes.

Navigate to the frontend folder.

Install dependencies:

Bash
npm install
Environment Config: Verify src/environments/environment.ts points to the correct API:

TypeScript
export const environment = {
  apiUrl: 'http://localhost:8080/api'
};
Run the application:

Bash
ng serve
Access the UI: Open http://localhost:4200 in your browser.

📋 Features
Product Selection: Fetches real-time insurance products from the backend.

Reactive Form: Includes validation for age (18-99), name length, and required fields.

Dynamic Pricing: The backend calculates premium totals based on basePrice * zoneMultiplier + ageSurcharge.

Quote History: A searchable and filterable list of all previously generated quotes.

🧪 Testing
Backend: Run mvn test to execute TESTS


🛡️ Troubleshooting
Empty Product Dropdown: Check the browser console (F12) for CORS errors. Ensure the @CrossOrigin import is present in Java controllers.

Connection Refused: Ensure the backend is fully started on port 8080 before launching the Angular app.